<%# One tenant, rendered the way /customers renders it: name AND id. The name
    is what an operator recognises; the id is what actually appears on API
    keys, Connect Links and tenant_id filters, so both are load-bearing.

    Every route that includes this fetches `tenants` non-fatally — an
    unreachable admin-api must not take an activity page down — so when the
    name cannot be resolved the bare id is shown rather than an em-dash: the
    id still identifies the tenant. Only a row with no tenant at all gets the
    dash.

    Included with `{ tenantId, tenants }` — see connections/index.ejs. (No
    literal example here: an EJS close tag inside this comment would end it.)
-%>
<% const _tenant = tenantId ? ((tenants || []).find((x) => x.id === tenantId) || null) : null -%>
<% if (tenantId) { %><% if (_tenant) { %><%= _tenant.name %> <% } %><span class="mono muted"><%= tenantId %></span><% } else { %><span class="muted">—</span><% } %>
